<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Gwalior State</h1><p class="st-meta">1731 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Lashkar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Maratha kingdom of the Scindias, whose ruler Mahadji Scindia controlled the Mughal emperor and which became one of the five 21-gun princely states.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranoji-scindia">Ranoji Scindia</a>, a commander under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a>, received a share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> around 1731, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Panipat</a> in 1761 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahadji-scindia">Mahadji Scindia</a> rebuilt the family's power with a European-trained army and from 1771 dominated the Mughal emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulat-rao-scindia">Daulat Rao Scindia</a> lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-maratha-war">Second Anglo-Maratha War</a> in 1803 and moved his military camp, Lashkar, to Gwalior. Defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maharajpur">Maharajpur</a> in 1843 brought British control over the army. In 1857 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayajirao-scindia">Jayajirao</a> stayed loyal, though his troops joined the rebels and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmibai">Rani of Jhansi</a> briefly took Gwalior fort. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madho-rao-scindia">Madho Rao</a> I built railways and industry, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-vilas-palace">Jai Vilas Palace</a> symbolised the dynasty's wealth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jivajirao-scindia">Jivajirao Scindia</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madhya-bharat">Madhya Bharat</a> in 1948, with Gwalior as its winter capital. The state joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956; the family remained prominent in Indian politics.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gwalior-state.jpg" alt="Gwalior State" width="320" height="174"><figcaption>Image: Wikijunkie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Gwalior_(State).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1731 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lashkar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ranoji Scindia, Mahadji Scindia, Jayajirao Scindia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hindi, Marathi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Scindia State, Gwalior</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=gwalior-state" aria-label="Gwalior State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6744 1215 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gwalior State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7064 1471L6991 1757M7064 1471L7364 1601M7064 1471L7364 1602M7064 1471L7064 1471"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6991 1757h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7064 1471h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7064 1471h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7064 1471h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=gwalior-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
